task: read-once
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/read-once
worktree: ../okay-wt-once

why: the first half of `queue-swap`'s channel-level version, and it
  stands on its own. Seven methods in `SentinelChannel` read the
  buffer more than once in a single operation. Today that is only a
  handful of extra field reads; the moment the buffer can be REPLACED
  under a running operation, two such reads can straddle the swap and
  compare a ring against a part of itself. Doing the audit first, with
  no behaviour change and the existing laws as the check, means the
  swap that follows is one small edit rather than one large one.

what: every method that touches the buffer reads it ONCE into a local
  and uses that local throughout. No semantic change, no new state,
  no new law — the existing suites are the proof, because any read
  that was load-bearing twice would break them.

predict: no measurable change (a field read the JIT already hoists),
  and 3 018 tests still green. Disqualifying: any law failing, which
  would mean one of those double reads was deliberate and needs a
  comment rather than a local.
